<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} routeId="builds" ogType="article" schema={[article]}><div class="shell page-wrap"><Breadcrumbs items={[{label:'DragonSword Guide',href:'/'},{label:'Builds'}]}/><article><header class="article-header"><p class="eyebrow">Build planning · Compare your setup</p><h1>DragonSword Awakening Build Planning</h1><LastVerified date="October 19, 2026" label="Guide updated"/></header>
<QuickAnswer title="Compare one change against the same encounter."><p>Write down your current setup and the problem you want to solve. Repeat the encounter with one change, then compare whether you can complete your intended attack sequence, survive, or finish more consistently. This is a planning checklist, not a tested character loadout or a best-build ranking.</p></QuickAnswer>
<div class="copy">
<h2>1. Choose the problem before changing the build</h2>
<ul><li><strong>A Signal does not appear:</strong> start with the receiving skill's written condition and your setup sequence. Use the <a href="/guides/combat-system/">Signal Skills guide</a> to check the handoff before changing equipment.</li><li><strong>You cannot finish the encounter:</strong> record where the attempt ends and which part of your sequence you can repeat. Use completion as your first comparison.</li><li><strong>You finish but want a faster clear:</strong> compare clear time only while keeping the encounter, difficulty and starting conditions the same.</li></ul>
<h2>2. Record a baseline, then change one thing</h2>
<p>The official combat overview connects Active Skills, Status Ailments and Signal Skills. For your own setup, copy the relevant skill text from your installed version rather than assuming every Hero can trigger the same follow-up.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Record setup A:</strong> game version, encounter and difficulty, Heroes, equipment, Runes, and the skill/switching sequence you actually use.</li><li><strong>Choose setup B:</strong> change one available option, such as a Rune or the switching order. Keep the other recorded choices fixed; if they also change, you cannot isolate which change helped.</li><li><strong>Repeat comparable attempts:</strong> note success or failure and the chosen outcome each time. Separate interrupted attempts or changed enemy conditions from the comparison.</li><li><strong>Choose the next step:</strong> keep B only if the improvement repeats for your chosen task. If results are mixed, keep the result inconclusive instead of calling B a stronger build.</li></ol>
<h2>3. Copy this comparison record into your notes</h2>
<ul><li>Version / encounter / difficulty:</li><li>Problem to solve:</li><li>Setup A — Heroes / equipment / Runes / sequence:</li><li>Setup B — the single change:</li><li>A attempts — completion / Signal sequence / clear time, as relevant:</li><li>B attempts — the same observations:</li><li>Changed conditions or interruptions:</li><li>Decision — keep A / keep B / inconclusive; reason:</li></ul>
<p>For example, if you change both a Rune and the switching order and the next attempt succeeds, record a successful attempt—not proof that the Rune caused it. Repeat with only one change before spending more resources on that assumption.</p>
<AdsterraNativeBanner/>
<h2>What this comparison can tell you</h2>
<p>Your notes can support a choice for the version, encounter and setup you tested. They do not establish a universal skill priority, best Rune set, Karma investment order or DPS ranking. Recheck the relevant skill descriptions when a game update changes them.</p>
<p>For the next question, use <a href="/guides/combat-system/">Signal Skills and missing prompts</a>, <a href="/systems/runes/">documented Rune system changes</a>, or the <a href="/teams/">team-composition overview</a>.</p>
<AdsterraBanner300x250/>
